To implement a system that alerts the inventory manager when a certain item is consistently going out of stock, you can use JavaScript to track inventory levels and set up alerts based on predefined thresholds. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create an array to store inventory data:
```javascript
const inventory = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Item A', quantity: 10 },
  { id: 2, name: 'Item B', quantity: 5 },
  { id: 3, name: 'Item C', quantity: 15 }
];
```
1. Define a function to update inventory quantities:
```javascript
function updateInventory(id, quantity) {
  const index = inventory.findIndex(item => item.id === id);
  if (index !== -1) {
    inventory[index].quantity = quantity;
  }
}
```
1. Define a function to check if an item is out of stock:
```javascript
function isOutOfStock(id) {
  const index = inventory.findIndex(item => item.id === id);
  return index !== -1 && inventory[index].quantity === 0;
}
```
1. Define a function to set up alerts for out-of-stock items:
```javascript
function setupAlerts() {
  const alertThreshold = 5; // Adjust this value based on your needs
  inventory.forEach(item => {
    if (isOutOfStock(item.id)) {
      console.log(`Alert: Item ${item.name} is out of stock`);
    } else if (item.quantity <= alertThreshold) {
      console.log(`Alert: Item ${item.name} is low in stock (quantity: ${item.quantity})`);
    }
  });
}
```
1. Call the `setupAlerts()` function periodically (e.g., every minute or hour) to check for out-of-stock items:
```javascript
setInterval(setupAlerts, 60000); // Check for alerts every minute
```

