To use a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator to reduce the noise in the image, you can add a few lines of code to your existing script. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filtered_image = fspecial('average', [3 3]);
gaussian_filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filtered_image);

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter to the input image using the `fspecial` function to create a Gaussian kernel and the `imfilter` function to apply the filter to the image. This helps to reduce the noise in the image before applying the Sobel operator.

Note that you can adjust the size of the Gaussian kernel (in this case, 3x3) and the type of filter (e.g., average, median, or Gaussian) based on your specific requirements.

