To identify the tense of a given sentence in MATLAB, we can use regular expressions and a simple rule-based approach. Here's a code snippet that demonstrates how to do this:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Define regular expressions for different tenses
    past_simple = 'had (.\*)';
    past_participle = 'been (.\*)';
    future_simple = 'will (.\*)';
    future_participle = 'going to (.\*)';
    present_simple = '(\w+) (in) the present';
    present_continuous = '(\w+) (being) in the present';
    present_perfect = 'have (.\*) (already)';
    present_perfect_continuous = 'have been (.\*) (for a while)';

    % Check if the sentence matches any of the regular expressions
    if regexp(sentence, past_simple, 'match')
        tense = 'Past Simple';
    elseif regexp(sentence, past_participle, 'match')
        tense = 'Past Participle';
    elseif regexp(sentence, future_simple, 'match')
        tense = 'Future Simple';
    elseif regexp(sentence, future_participle, 'match')
        tense = 'Future Participle';
    elseif regexp(sentence, present_simple, 'match')
        tense = 'Present Simple';
    elseif regexp(sentence, present_continuous, 'match')
        tense = 'Present Continuous';
    elseif regexp(sentence, present_perfect, 'match')
        tense = 'Present Perfect';
    elseif regexp(sentence, present_perfect_continuous, 'match')
        tense = 'Present Perfect Continuous';
    else
        tense = 'Other';
    end
end

