To divide an hour into 10 equal parts, you can use the following formula in Excel:

`=TIME(HOUR(A1), (MINUTE(A1) / 6), 0)`

This formula takes the hour and minute values from cell A1 and divides the minute value by 6 to get the fractional part of the hour. It then adds this fractional part to the hour value to get the time in the first 10-minute interval. You can drag this formula down to apply it to the rest of the hourly data.

In MATLAB, you can use the following code to achieve the same result:

```matlab
function divided_time = divide_hour(hour, minute)
    fractional_part = minute / 6;
    divided_time = TIME(hour, fractional_part, 0);
end
```

You can call this function with your hour and minute values as arguments to get the divided time. For example:

```matlab
hour = 14;
minute = 30;
divided_time = divide_hour(hour, minute);
disp(divided_time);
```

This will output the time in the first 10-minute interval for the given hour and minute values.

