Yes, you can adjust the layout of widgets in the WordPress admin (WP-Admin) by using the built-in Widgets screen or with the help of custom CSS. Here's how to do it:

Method 1: Using the Widgets Screen

1. Log in to your WordPress dashboard and go to "Appearance" > "Widgets".
2. Drag and drop the widgets you want to use into the desired areas on the right side of the screen. You can also resize the widgets by clicking and dragging the bottom right corner.
3. To create custom widget areas, click on the "Add a Widget Area" button at the bottom of the screen. Give your widget area a name and then drag and drop widgets into it.
4. Once you have arranged the widgets to your liking, click on the "Save" button at the bottom of the screen to apply your changes.

Method 2: Using Custom CSS

If you prefer to use custom CSS to adjust the layout of widgets, follow these steps:

1. Log in to your WordPress dashboard and go to "Appearance" > "Editor".
2. In the "Theme Files" section, find the file named "style.css" or "styles.css".
3. Add the following CSS code to the end of the file:
```css
/* Adjust the width of widgets */
.widget {
    width: 100%; /* Set the width of each widget to 100% */
}

/* Adjust the spacing between widgets */
.widget + .widget {
    margin-top: 10px; /* Add a 10px margin between adjacent widgets */
}

/* Create custom widget areas */
.widget-area {
    padding: 20px; /* Add some padding to the widget area */
}
```
1. Save the file by clicking on the "Update File" button at the bottom of the screen.
2. Now, you can adjust the width and spacing of widgets by changing the values in the CSS code. For example, if you want to increase the space between widgets, you can increase the value of the `margin-top` property.

Remember to replace the sample CSS code with your own custom CSS rules as needed.

In WordPress, you can customize the layout of widgets on your site's dashboard by using CSS. To do this, follow these steps:

1. Create a new file named `dashboard.css` in your theme's `css` folder. If you don't have a `css` folder, create one and add it to your theme.
2. Add the following code to the `dashboard.css` file:
```css
/* Hide the default WordPress dashboard widgets */
.wp-dashboard .widget {
    display: none;
}

/* Customize the layout of your own widgets */
.your-widget-class {
    width: 30%;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
}

.another-widget-class {
    width: 45%;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
}
```
Replace `.your-widget-class` and `.another-widget-class` with the actual class names of your desired widgets. You can also adjust the width and margin values to suit your needs.

3. Enqueue the `dashboard.css` file in your theme's `functions.php` file or use a plugin like "Additional CSS" to add the CSS code to your site. Here's an example of how to enqueue the `dashboard.css` file in your theme's `functions.php` file:
```php
function my_theme_enqueue_styles() {
    wp_enqueue_style('my-theme-dashboard', get_template_directory_uri() . '/css/dashboard.css', array(), '1.0', 'all');
}
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', 'my_theme_enqueue_styles');
```
Now, your customized widget layout will be applied to the WP admin dashboard. Note that this method will hide the default WordPress dashboard widgets, so if you want to keep some of them, you'll need to modify the CSS selectors accordingly.

