To call a PowerShell script from within Terraform and allow a `Read-Host` input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_host.ps1`) that takes a parameter and uses `Read-Host` to get input:

```powershell
param(
    [string]$input
)

Write-Host "Input: $input"
$output = Read-Host "Enter some text:"
Write-Host "Output: $output"
```

2. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), use the `local-exec` provisioner to call the PowerShell script and pass the input as a variable:

```hcl
provider "null" {
  version = "~> 3.0"
}

resource "null_resource" "read_host" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -File read_host.ps1 -input 'Hello, world!'"
  }
}
```

In this example, the `local-exec` provisioner is used to run the `read_host.ps1` script with the input set to "Hello, world!". The output of the script will be printed to the console.

Note that you may need to adjust the path to the PowerShell script depending on where it's located. Also, make sure that you have PowerShell installed on the machine running Terraform.

