To achieve this, you can use PowerShell along with the ActiveDirectory module. Here's a script that will recursively check folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory:

```powershell
# Load the ActiveDirectory module
Import-Module ActiveDirectory

# Define the function to check ACLs for SIDHistory references
function CheckACLsForSIDHistory($folderPath) {
    # Get the folder's ACLs
    $acls = (Get-Acl -Path $folderPath).Access | Where-Object {$_.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21'}

    # Loop through each ACL and check for SIDHistory references
    foreach ($acl in $acls) {
        $sid = $acl.IdentityReference
        $sidHistory = (Get-ADUser -Filter "SamAccountName -eq '$sid'" -Properties MemberOf).MemberOf

        if ($sidHistory) {
            Write-Host "Folder: $folderPath"
            Write-Host "SID: $sid"
            Write-Host "SIDHistory: $sidHistory"
        }
    }
}

# Define the root folder to start the recursion
$rootFolder = "C:\Your_Root_Folder"

# Recursively check the folders
Get-ChildItem -Path $rootFolder -Directory | ForEach-Object {
    CheckACLsForSIDHistory($_.FullName)
    Get-ChildItem -Path $_.FullName -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        CheckACLsForSIDHistory($_.FullName)
    }
}
```

Replace `C:\Your_Root_Folder` with the root folder you want to start the recursion from. This script will output any folders with ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory.
